A||||7||XDrewX||what is story behind dif tire sizes||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||06-07-2001||11:44 PM||||I am about to purchase new rime and tires for my 87 gt, does anybody know why the reaer tires are larger, and if i must maintain the tire size difference? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||saleenfiero||06-07-2001||11:58 PM||||On performance cars, they have bigger rear tire for obvious reasons.  Also, if you put tires too fat on the front, it will hamper handling.  I just got new rims, I had 235/60/14 tire on the back at first and now 215/45/17 all around and it seems to drag less, maybe no difference at all, but it does seem more nimble (not just from less sidewall)  With fieros it all just seems to depend on preference, if you want the a$$end filled out a bit, then go for it, put fatties on, otherwise, save yourself the confusion and buy all the same width.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-08-2001||01:31 AM||||Originally the fastback GT tires were 2 different sizes because the shape of the body. They put slightly larger tires in back to give it a slight rake look. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||06-08-2001||08:21 AM||||Having wider tires on the back can help correct a tendency to oversteer also.  Pretty common on cars with rear or mid engine layout and a rear weight bias like the 911.||||reg||1
Z||000004||montage||06-08-2001||09:01 AM||||To take what Wampus Cat said a bit further one way to tune your suspension is by varying the widths of the front and rear tires. If I remember correctly (and very basically), if the front has a larger slip angle you will get understeer and if the rear has a larger slip angle you will get oversteer (don't forget that weight also plays a large role in addition to several other factors). By making the tires wider in the rear the slip angle is less, smaller slip angle in the rear more understeer (and visa versa). Additionally, the sway bar stiffness (springs too) also affect slip angle so a stiffer bar makes a smaller cornering force and therefore the slip angle gets smaller. One shouldn't forget that acceleration and braking in a corner can also change cornering force, etc., etc., etc……….||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||06-08-2001||09:51 AM||||Good info.<BR>One thing to remmeber about sway bars and such, going to a mild bar from no bar at all can help reduce slip angles and improve grip on that end, but a big bar (or a high setting on an adjustable bar) can limit suspension travel too much and therefore all momentum will transfer directly to the tires and you will slip sooner. <BR>I used an adjustable Neuspeed bar on the rear of my '97 GTI VR6. For autocross I would set it stiffer to increase oversteer, helping the front-driver get around tighter courses. <BR>It's a matter of finding the "sweet spot" for your setup, and adjusting from there. If you're set too high, reducing the bar will actually help. <BR>Same goes for tire pressures. From the sweet spot, if you raise pressure, you will slide more with that end, lowering pressure will have an opposite effect, until you get out of the "sweet spot", then you will get too much sidewall roll and slide anyway.<BR>Shock tuning mirrors these effects, as do spring rates.<BR>At any rate, it's all relative for your setup, and no 2 cars are exactly alike. The brand, compound and condition of your tires will have a HUGE effect, and the options on the car (which springs, shocks, strutbars, engine, trans, clutch...)and the condition of each, will vary your results. <BR>A good long weekend of autocross has always been the best way I have found to setup a car. This is why most SCCA clubs have a "test and tune" weekend (no points) as their first gathering for the year. <P>Hope some of this helps...I know it was slightly off topic, but it relates. <BR>Good luck finding your car's setup. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steve Normington||06-08-2001||11:06 AM||||What exactly is slip angle?||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRiAD||06-08-2001||11:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>What exactly is slip angle?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>SLIP ANGLE  <P>Definition: The angle between the direction in which a tire is aimed or steered and the actual direction of tire travel. <P>From <A HREF="http://autorepair.about.com/autos/autorepair/library/glossary/blglossary.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://autorepair.about.com/autos/autorepair/library/glossary/blglossary.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
